00:04:48.425 --> 00:04:49.300
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.758
So we said a lot
of products there.

00:04:50.758 --> 00:04:52.990
But this is probably
a very big question,

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.182
but what is machine learning?

00:04:56.182 --> 00:04:56.890
YUFENG GUO: Yeah.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:00.760
So machine learning--
the what is it question

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:04.320
is definitely a big one
that, kind of, everybody's

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.550
wondering right now.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.820
And in general, I--
my short version,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.630
of what is machine
learning, is--

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.770
I define it with
five or six words.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:18.060
It's using examples
to answer questions,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:22.170
or answering questions
using many examples.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.850
So I think that's five words.

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.560
So then all of-- and I've
distilled it down to that,

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:27.060
you know.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.820
So all the words are
kind of important.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.990
Using-- you want to
answer questions.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.690
We're not just using machine
learning for the sake of it.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:37.800
It's the purpose of it is to
answer the questions that you

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.190
have, and then we want to
use examples to do that.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:45.360
So that means you need to have
examples to show your system,

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:46.950
and you need lots of them.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.570
So questions.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.640
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
instead of, basically like,

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.980
writing an algorithm,
what you do is you just

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.560
throw data at something.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.310
Like this is how you're
supposed to do it,

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.620
and the machine learns,
that's the machine

00:05:58.620 --> 00:05:59.680
learning part of it.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.950
YUFENG GUO: Right, and
another way to kind

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:05.400
of visualize that is to think
of traditional programming,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.370
as you writing a bunch of rules.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.040
You can think of it almost like
the flow of a river, right.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.890
So the information flows
from the top of your program

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:16.050
downwards, goes to
different functions,

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.690
flows through if
statements and form loops.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.840
And then the output of
your various functions,

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.620
and your program does something.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.050
Right, so--

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.850
A machine learning
algorithm is kind

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.020
of doing the same
thing with data.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.520
You're writing, you're
creating some structure,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.170
and all of this data
is flowing through

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.910
and all this math is happening.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.130
And then on the other side
of that there's some output.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:41.670
But there is this
additional step beforehand,

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.832
where you need to write code
to make that something, where

00:06:44.832 --> 00:06:45.790
the data flows through.

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:47.640
So you need to
make the structures

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.370
that the data will flow
through, in the first place.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.800
Whereas, with normal
programming that is your code.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.008
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:06:54.008 --> 00:06:57.330
So given all of that
context, where is TensorFlow.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:58.080
YUFENG GUO: Right.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.230
So TensorFlow then is
an open source library

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.660
that Google released a
little over a year ago,

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.760
in November of 2015.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.250
And it's an open source library
written at its core in c++

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:15.980
and c, and then has a Python
API and now a couple of APIs

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:17.170
in other languages.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There's a
go-- there's a go buy me.

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:19.010
YUFENG GUO: Yes.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:20.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: [LAUGHING]

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.950
YUFENG GUO: And so
that's a library

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:27.372
for facilitating the
creation of these structures,

00:07:27.372 --> 00:07:28.830
that we were talking
about earlier,

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.204
that the data
would flow through.

00:07:30.204 --> 00:07:31.320
We call them graphs.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.070
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:35.226
So all right, say I'm
like, yes machine learning

00:07:35.226 --> 00:07:37.350
and TensorFlow, but I don't
know where to apply it.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.657
What are some common, or
like, regular type things

00:07:40.657 --> 00:07:42.990
that people tend to do with
machine learning that people

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:44.740
could then apply it themselves?

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:49.230
YUFENG GUO: So the two or three
big categories of use cases

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:55.290
that we're seeing emerge is
image classification, data--

00:07:55.290 --> 00:08:00.420
structured data classification
or regression, and finally,

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.380
something to the
effect of, time series.

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:07.080
So whether it's streaming
log data or videos or audio,

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.077
the time series where you care
about the previous data points,

00:08:10.077 --> 00:08:11.910
those are kind of the
three broad categories

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:12.520
that we're seeing.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:13.950
MARK MANDEL: So when
you say classification,

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.658
what does that actually
mean though, just

00:08:15.658 --> 00:08:17.190
so people understand 100%.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:17.940
YUFENG GUO: Right.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.020
So classification is
where you put the data

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.730
into, kind of, different
buckets, different categories,

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:25.560
if you will.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.410
And you could have as
few as two categories,

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:31.860
and those two could even be--

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:33.570
is it x?

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:34.909
Or is it not x?

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:37.150
It doesn't necessarily
have to be x and y.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:40.200
Or it could be like, I'm
recognizing these pictures

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.610
as apples or other.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:46.200
Doesn't necessarily have
to be apples or bananas.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:49.650
And so the
classification has also

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.830
emerged as this place
where you end up

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:55.452
being able to put a lot
of problem sets into.

00:08:55.452 --> 00:08:56.910
Like, you can fit
a lot of problems

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:00.460
into "the domain of
classification", quote unquote,

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.540
because you can also go
from not just two classes,

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.470
but many, many classes.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:10.862
So you can, say, have 100
classes or even 1,000 classes.

00:09:10.862 --> 00:09:12.570
So then you begin to
get into a situation

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:16.290
where you're able
to bucket things

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.510
into many, many
different buckets,

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:20.430
and that can also
be very powerful.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:22.096
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Can
you give an example

00:09:22.096 --> 00:09:24.870
of-- like when you're saying
like 1,000 buckets, what kind

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:26.220
of application would do that?

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.410
YUFENG GUO: So one
classic example

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.580
is the Imagenet
Competition, which

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:32.580
is an international machine
learning competition

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.010
for image recognition.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:40.740
So they provide 1,000
categories that you

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.200
as the machine learning
scientists, or the competitor,

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.400
need to create a machine
learning model which

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.490
will classify the input
data, or the pictures,

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:55.210
into the correct one of
these 1,000 categories.

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:57.900
So if you get a picture of
a specific breed of dog,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.800
that might be a particular
category or bucket

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.242
in those thousands.

00:10:02.242 --> 00:10:03.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:07.232
So what about when you have
something that is not really--

00:10:07.232 --> 00:10:09.690
I'd say it's probably not a
classification problem, because

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:13.770
rather than having
a bunch of buckets,

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:17.330
however many you might have,
it is something continuous.

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:18.660
Like-- I don't know.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:21.630
Like, it's something-- you're
trying to predict a value,

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:24.790
and it's a flow so, you know,
there's infinite technically.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.730
Well, technically
not, but whatever.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:28.500
YUFENG GUO:
Absolutely, and so that

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.470
is what is typically
referred to as regression.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:35.310
So that's the other side
of this particular subset

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:39.120
of problems, where you are
trying to predict the value.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:42.990
One example, that kind of
treads the line, is let's

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.820
say you had a machine
learning model

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.970
that took a bunch of inputs,
such as your diet, when

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:48.980
you went to sleep--

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:53.820
there's lots of these quantified
self tracking variables--

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.687
and tried to predict your age.

00:10:55.687 --> 00:10:56.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.103
I thought you were
going to say trying

00:10:58.103 --> 00:10:59.996
to predict the day you die.

00:10:59.996 --> 00:11:00.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:01.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cause then--

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:01.940
MARK MANDEL: It's
the same thing.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.905
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
just that [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:03.905 --> 00:11:05.780
YUFENG GUO: I'm pretty
sure, the day you die,

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:08.700
that's probably just a
random generator, x time

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:09.670
in the future.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:13.470
So with that, what's tricky
about it is if you just

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.600
output a number, like
an integer number,

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:18.690
do you count that as
regression or classification?

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.370
Because, yeah, there
are a lot of ages,

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.360
right, one through something
on the order of 100.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.990
But as we said, you could
pretty easily classify it

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:28.660
between 100 different things.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:30.900
And maybe you say,
well I don't think

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:32.940
I can be as precise as
to say like, you know,

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:37.851
your age is 35.7 or
something like that.

00:11:37.851 --> 00:11:39.600
But at the same time,
maybe you don't even

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.150
want to be as specific
as saying it's 35.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:45.690
So then you get into saying
bucketing into different age

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:49.480
groups, and saying, OK you're
in the 18 to 25 age group.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:51.870
And you got to think about
like surveys and things

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:53.370
like that operate in a sense.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:54.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
there's basically--

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:55.950
like there's
classification problems,

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.570
there's regression
problems, and then

00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:59.153
something kind of
in the middle, where

00:11:59.153 --> 00:12:01.215
you could choose going
one way or the other.

00:12:01.215 --> 00:12:05.252
Are all of those problems
treatable with TensorFlow.

00:12:05.252 --> 00:12:06.210
YUFENG GUO: Absolutely.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:07.060
Absolutely.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:10.290
TensorFlow is really meant
to be a general purpose

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:11.610
machine learning library.

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:14.580
And it's actually,
in some ways, broader

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.760
than that, because
all it's really doing

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.964
is creating this
graph, that we were

00:12:19.964 --> 00:12:22.380
talking about earlier, where
all the numbers flow through.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.089
And you can create that
to do anything you want.

00:12:25.089 --> 00:12:26.880
You can have it do
math, just like addition

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.970
or multiplication, and
then compute, you know,

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:33.840
other kind of more
complicated math things

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.152
that are not machine learning.

00:12:35.152 --> 00:12:36.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:36.770
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.145
YUFENG GUO: That being said,
yeah, the high level tooling

00:12:39.145 --> 00:12:41.040
around it that says
like, you know,

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.351
just make this thing for me.

00:12:42.351 --> 00:12:44.100
That's all geared
toward machine learning.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:47.100
But if you go into it and
you use some of the low level

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.530
tooling, where you can
really control the graph

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.797
and build it kind of from
scratch piece by piece,

00:12:51.797 --> 00:12:53.130
you can build anything you want.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:53.880
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:54.799
So I got a question.

00:12:54.799 --> 00:12:55.840
So Americans are curious.

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:57.450
So we've seen examples,
like you said,

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:59.970
like where image classification
is a classification problem.

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:04.560
But like Francesc talked about
Martin's talk last episode,

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.860
and in that he was generating
stuff out of models

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:09.262
that he put TensorFlow into.

00:13:09.262 --> 00:13:11.470
Like, basically, making
things sound like Shakespeare

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:12.900
or Shakespearean like text.

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:15.150
Where does that
kind of sit, where

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:17.190
it's like you're
generating stuff to seem

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:18.350
like similar other things.

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:20.790
Is that like predictive,
or what's the deal there?

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:21.998
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:21.998 --> 00:13:27.450
So these generative models is
kind of a new, newish class

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.240
of machine learning--

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.130
algorithm machine
learning models.

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:37.230
And part of it is mapped to
the kind of traditional things

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:40.290
that we were talking about,
where you have this model--

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:43.620
in this particular case,
you're taking all the works

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:47.550
of Shakespeare, and putting
it, running through the model

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:49.500
so that the output
is this model that

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:53.980
can recognize whether something
is or isn't Shakespeare.

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.800
So that you could
give it some new text,

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.150
and then it will think of--
you know, run it through,

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.990
and then on the other side it'll
say, yes this is Shakespeare,

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:02.280
or no it's not Shakespeare.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.200
Now in doing that,
you've basically

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:09.930
created a little system that
can also generate Shakespearean.

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:12.660
It knows what Shakespeare
is like, right.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:14.850
It's encoded into that model.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:18.270
And so then if you could
massage it in the right way

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:23.890
you can also have it output what
it thinks Shakespeare would be.

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:26.580
And another way-- another way
to think about or design that

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:31.860
would be to, say, give
it Shakespeare, and have

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:34.215
it learn-- this is more
of that sequencing model

00:14:34.215 --> 00:14:36.590
that we were talking about,
the third kind of class where

00:14:36.590 --> 00:14:38.680
you're looking at a
sequence of words.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:40.815
So with Shakespeare
you have any writing

00:14:40.815 --> 00:14:41.940
that's a sequence of words.

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:44.400
So you teach it
to say, OK I know

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:49.830
that after word a usually
there's either words b1, b2,

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:53.025
or b3, and then it's like c1,
c2, c3-- you know, these words.

00:14:53.025 --> 00:14:54.900
And so these are like
the possible sequences.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:58.170
So given some input it
can kind of continue.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:01.020
And that's the exact
same idea and basis

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:03.860
for some of the audio models
that we see, like the music

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:06.350
related ones where you can
play the first few notes

00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:08.150
and then it picks up for you.

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:10.880
It's just analyzing
the data that's

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:13.850
embedded in the music
signal, whether that's

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:16.122
just straight audio or
like something like MIDI.

00:15:16.122 --> 00:15:17.330
MARK MANDEL: All right, cool.

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:19.663
So we've talked about TensorFlow
through the open source

00:15:19.663 --> 00:15:20.240
project.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:23.030
Let's talk about Cloud
Machine Learning Engine,

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:24.239
and like, what is that thing.

00:15:24.239 --> 00:15:26.154
So can you tell us like
what's the difference,

00:15:26.154 --> 00:15:27.740
or what is the Cloud
Machine Learning

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:29.581
Engine from a high level?

00:15:29.581 --> 00:15:31.580
YUFENG GUO: So the Cloud
Machine Learning Engine

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:35.570
is really, at its core,
managed infrastructure

00:15:35.570 --> 00:15:39.710
for running TensorFlow on
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:43.010
And so we'll break down
each of those points.

00:15:43.010 --> 00:15:46.460
The part about it being
managed infrastructure,

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:49.610
the difference there
is it's not that you

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:52.910
need to spin up some
virtual machines,

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:56.330
allocate some storage
or persistent disk,

00:15:56.330 --> 00:16:00.110
and then tell it, oh here's
where I want you to run things.

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:01.500
Right, it will do that for you.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:02.730
It will spin up the machines.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:07.040
It will make sure the right
libraries are installed etc.

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:09.750
And so you have this
managed infrastructure,

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:13.040
and you then just have to
give it your TensorFlow code.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:15.770
And we have the G
Cloud command line

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:17.930
tool that will take care
of that for you as well.

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:21.710
The code goes up, and then
the machines are automatically

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:25.974
created for you, spun up,
and your code is loaded in.

00:16:25.974 --> 00:16:27.140
And then it runs it for you.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:29.450
And then when it's
done, that output

00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:32.500
goes to Google Cloud Storage
where your data was as well.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.910
And so you can define
all of those parameters,

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:37.137
including how many
machines, how powerful you

00:16:37.137 --> 00:16:39.470
want those machines to be,
whether those machines should

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:42.290
have GPU attached to them etc.

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.840
But at the end of the
day, the code that you

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:46.610
write, the machine
learning code,

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.304
is just pure TensorFlow code.

00:16:48.304 --> 00:16:49.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:52.250
So you mentioned the output.

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:53.480
What is the output?

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.760
Is that the prediction that
you make, or what is it?

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:59.540
YUFENG GUO: Usually,
in the first half

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:01.390
of the work that
you do, which is you

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:05.069
are training, the output of
that is the model itself.

00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:07.670
So that's just a
file or a few files

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:12.770
that together define
that graph of, basically,

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:17.089
that mathematical kind of
model that can take an input

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:20.147
and generate the desired
output, usually a prediction.

00:17:20.147 --> 00:17:20.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:22.790
So that's what all
the people [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:25.700
like a neural network itself?

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:27.140
Is that what we're
talking about?

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:27.973
YUFENG GUO: Exactly.

00:17:27.973 --> 00:17:30.630
So in the case of a neural
network, that file would just

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.170
be lots and lots of
numbers in its path

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:35.900
down into a binary
format, and you know,

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:37.970
you can pass in a picture
and it will tell you,

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:40.610
is it a kitten or
a puppy, right.

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:44.030
And so that first
step is just half

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:47.090
of the machine learning engine,
in that that's the training

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:47.780
half.

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:50.920
And the other half
of machine learning

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:53.610
is the answering of questions.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:57.530
And so to facilitate that, you
have the prediction service.

00:17:57.530 --> 00:18:00.380
So that's what I view as
another large chunk of it that

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:02.960
is often overlooked, because
so much emphasis is placed

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:06.770
on the training, because it is
the more challenging half by

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:07.730
and large.

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:10.520
But after you've
gathered all your data,

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:14.750
cleaned it, picked the right
model, tuned as parameters,

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:17.780
spent who knows how long
training it and waiting

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:20.690
for it to finish, the last
thing you want to be doing

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:24.770
is then going and looking up on
Stack Overflow, how exactly do

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:27.980
I spin up a web server
to wrap this model

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:33.050
to scale that server, logging,
quotas, all the good stuff.

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:35.840
Especially for someone who
is well-versed in machine

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:39.310
learning, that tends to be
your data scientist or machine

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:43.090
learning researcher,
they are oftentimes not

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:45.935
as experienced in
managing infrastructure.

00:18:45.935 --> 00:18:48.560
They might not, because they're
spending all their time in kind

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.140
of machine learning land.

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:53.030
They're not over here learning
about Kubernetes, and Docker,

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:55.070
and scalable systems,
and things like that.

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:56.990
So the prediction service
helps you with that.

00:18:56.990 --> 00:19:00.620
It says, just give us the model,
that folder with the couple

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:02.374
files that was output
from your training--

00:19:02.374 --> 00:19:04.790
MARK MANDEL: And it gives you
a rest endpoint or something

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:05.340
like that?

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:07.381
YUFENG GUO: Exactly, it
gives you a scalable rest

00:19:07.381 --> 00:19:09.530
endpoint that is
deployed for you,

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:12.320
and it also takes
care of versioning,

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:13.940
so that you can
say, OK I'm going

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:17.510
to make a model, my
puppies or kittens model.

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:20.150
And this is going to be v1.

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:23.060
And then later when
you turn a new model,

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:24.170
you can say this is v2.

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:26.150
And you have both of them, and
you can switch between them

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:27.310
as like which ones default.

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:29.400
MARK MANDEL: It's like App
Engine and Points merge

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:30.650
together for machine learning.

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:32.025
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, Spherical.

00:19:32.025 --> 00:19:34.640
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, so I think
that's a really neat feature

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:38.000
in that those two
steps are decoupled,

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.720
thanks to TensorFlow, in that
you have this model's file--

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:43.520
model file output.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:47.240
Which means that if
you do local training,

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:50.150
you output that same exact file,
because Cloud Machine Learning

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.659
Engine is just
running TensorFlow.

00:19:52.659 --> 00:19:54.200
The outputs of
training in the cloud,

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:56.380
and outputs of training
on your laptop,

00:19:56.380 --> 00:19:57.600
are pretty much the same.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:19:59.030
It's just a question of, do
you want it to be distributed?

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:00.900
Do you want it to be faster?

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:02.690
And so that output,
then you can just

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:05.570
push or upload those
files to the cloud

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:07.940
yourself, and then put
it to the prediction.

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:10.129
And so you could train
locally and predict

00:20:10.129 --> 00:20:11.420
in a scalable way on the cloud.

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:14.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So I see the value

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:18.260
of serving those models,
that prediction on the cloud,

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:20.270
because, you know, you
want people to access it.

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:22.790
What is the benefit of
training on the cloud,

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:24.500
rather than training
on your own laptop?

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:26.250
YUFENG GUO: That's a
really good question.

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:28.410
So when training
on the cloud, there

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:30.410
are a couple of reasons
you might want to do it,

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:31.340
on both sides, right.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:34.910
If you're just working with a
small data set, and you know,

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:37.320
you're OK with the
performance on your laptop,

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:40.400
then by all means train on your
laptop, train on your desktop,

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:43.580
train on your local GPU
cluster, if you have one, right.

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:45.650
But when you get
into situations where

00:20:45.650 --> 00:20:52.070
your data sizes are too big to
be stored on your one machine--

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:54.470
and that can be a number
of different thresholds,

00:20:54.470 --> 00:20:57.590
because you know, if I
just have my laptop here;

00:20:57.590 --> 00:21:00.330
on my laptop maybe it just
has one terabyte of disk.

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:02.360
And so something
less than a terabyte,

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:04.610
but more than a couple
of hundred gigs,

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:07.790
I probably don't want to
be wrangling that around.

00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:10.160
Maybe what I'll do,
though, is take a section.

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:13.310
I'll take a representative
sample of that data.

00:21:13.310 --> 00:21:15.770
Put that on my laptop,
and train that locally,

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:17.680
so I can get the code
working correctly.

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:19.840
That's a nice and
fast iteration cycle.

00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:21.715
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It was
like your development

00:21:21.715 --> 00:21:22.820
cycle is on the computer.

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:23.060
YUFENG GUO: Right.

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:24.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
then when you actually

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:26.300
train, you will not
turn on your computer,

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:28.097
because it's too slow or--

00:21:28.097 --> 00:21:30.680
YUFENG GUO: So usually, yeah,
if your data set is really quite

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:33.520
large, it can be too slow.

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:35.137
MARK MANDEL: What is large?

00:21:35.137 --> 00:21:36.470
Can you give us a rule of thumb.

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:38.870
I know obviously ballpark
figuresish, but where would

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:40.707
you say that line
would be in terms of--

00:21:40.707 --> 00:21:41.540
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:43.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How many
kittens versus puppies

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:44.060
are we talking about here?

00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:44.570
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:45.695
YUFENG GUO: Yeah, because--

00:21:45.695 --> 00:21:48.690
and before I try to come
up with a number here,

00:21:48.690 --> 00:21:51.890
there's so many other parameters
that play around that data,

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:54.350
because it depends on
how big your network is

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:56.540
that you're trying to train.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:59.022
How big are these images
that you're pushing in?

00:21:59.022 --> 00:22:00.980
Whether or not you're
going to downsampled them

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:04.520
to a lower resolution will
also make a big difference.

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:08.720
And then finally just your
general hardware and ops

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:11.027
configurations around
transfer speed.

00:22:11.027 --> 00:22:12.860
So a lot of times you
end up with situations

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:14.240
where you're IO bound.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:16.790
Sometimes it's compute bound,
and then sometimes it's

00:22:16.790 --> 00:22:19.670
kind of like memory bound, just
like shuffling things around.

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:22.250
There are situations
where people

00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:28.880
have had to move to distributed
CPUs, because the data could

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:33.665
not fit on the 6 gigs or 12
gigs of RAM on the GPU card.

00:22:33.665 --> 00:22:34.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wow.

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:35.066
OK.

00:22:35.066 --> 00:22:37.190
YUFENG GUO: Like they had
to hold all that at once.

00:22:37.190 --> 00:22:39.106
I'm not saying that the
whole data set was big

00:22:39.106 --> 00:22:41.420
and that, because of course
a lot of times they are.

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:43.320
But like for the computation

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:44.590
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:46.900
--hold, you know, over
six or 12 gigs of data.

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:47.960
It's like, that's crazy.

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:50.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you
didn't answer the question.

00:22:50.290 --> 00:22:51.509
ALL: [LAUGHING]

00:22:51.509 --> 00:22:52.550
MARK MANDEL: Nicely done.

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:53.716
FRANCESC CAMPOY: From your--

00:22:53.716 --> 00:22:54.220
Right, yeah.

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:55.770
Very developer advocate.

00:22:55.770 --> 00:23:00.560
But so give us that one concrete
example that you've done.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:01.970
How big was the data?

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:05.720
And how long it took to
train on your laptop,

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:08.570
or how long it took to
train on Cloud ML engine?

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:11.650
YUFENG GUO: So one
good example of that

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:16.490
was when I was training a
transfer learning model for--

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:19.020
actually, I was running
through one of our code labs.

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:22.280
It was for image
model for recognizing

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:25.590
five different types
of flowers, and--

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:27.929
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is more than I can.

00:23:27.929 --> 00:23:29.720
YUFENG GUO: They were
really common flowers

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:32.250
like roses and tulips and
lilies, and things like that.

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:37.660
So that data set was I
believe it was 200,000--

00:23:37.660 --> 00:23:38.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Images?

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:39.451
YUFENG GUO: Images.

00:23:39.451 --> 00:23:40.340
They were images.

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:45.470
And training locally,
my CPU it was

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:48.887
taking on the order
of like half an hour.

00:23:48.887 --> 00:23:51.470
So it was getting to the point
I was like, well I don't really

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.900
want to be burnt taking this.

00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:55.640
I can't use my
browser, it's slow,

00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:58.890
it's burning from my
battery, the fan is spinning.

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:01.040
And so pushing
that to the cloud,

00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:04.370
I was able-- it was coming
down to, not including

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:05.540
kind of spin up time.

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:08.660
And that's the other
thing I will get into.

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:11.210
It was taking about
like 15ish minutes,

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:13.760
so half-- but there was
like not like that powerful

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:14.814
of a configuration.

00:24:14.814 --> 00:24:16.730
You know, I think it was
just like, basically,

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:17.900
a fancy desktop.

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:20.360
And so moving from the
laptop to a fancy desktop,

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:25.110
you know, you've got some boost,
but you know, nothing crazy.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:27.650
I think the crazy
scaling factors

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:31.610
happen when you have truly
tremendous data sets.

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:33.756
You know, maybe
on the order of--

00:24:33.756 --> 00:24:34.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Terabytes.

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:36.171
YUFENG GUO: Possibly terabytes.

00:24:36.171 --> 00:24:38.450
Like hundreds of gigs
to multiple terabytes

00:24:38.450 --> 00:24:39.920
worth of data and up.

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:43.400
And the data size is a really
hard number to really just go

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:46.830
against, because if you have
image data, it's bigger.

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:49.060
If you have logs files,
it will be smaller,

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:55.070
and you can fit a lot of
log files in 10,000 jpgs.

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:56.840
And those log files
are rich data.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:58.460
And so pushing
that all through--

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:01.940
and if it's a complex model
can also take a long time.

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:05.624
So it's not a easy
kind of rule of thumb.

00:25:05.624 --> 00:25:06.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:11.600
So I'm curious about, so,
TensorFlow, what it is.

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:13.192
It's an open source
library, right?

00:25:13.192 --> 00:25:13.900
YUFENG GUO: Yeah.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:17.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is not
the only one, I'm assuming.

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:20.310
What is-- like could you give
a little bit of a comparison?

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:23.890
Why would you use TensorFlow
rather than something else?

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:26.540
And I personally
don't know any other.

00:25:26.540 --> 00:25:29.370
So I'm going to let you
answer that question.

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:33.590
YUFENG GUO: So with TensorFlow
the advantages there

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:36.530
I think are primarily
around the ability

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:40.190
to go from research
to production easily.

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:42.080
In the past and in a
lot of other fields

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:46.010
as well, where you have
research and production as kind

00:25:46.010 --> 00:25:49.470
of separate parts
of the industry,

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:52.100
you end up with a situation
where the research is

00:25:52.100 --> 00:25:55.280
doing things in one language
or writing their own custom

00:25:55.280 --> 00:25:56.600
frameworks.

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:59.570
And then to implement
it, to make it run fast,

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:02.270
you rewrite everything in c++.

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:04.430
And what's great about
TensorFlow is that

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:07.500
the underlying implementation
is already in c++,

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:11.210
but you get to work on it via
Python, or in your case Go.

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:13.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I have
not tried it yet, but yeah.

00:26:13.490 --> 00:26:17.990
YUFENG GUO: So that allows
you to have flexibility

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:21.190
while maintaining
high performance.

00:26:21.190 --> 00:26:24.690
And one of the things in
the TensorFlow 1.0 release,

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:27.890
which came out in
February 15th, is

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:32.210
the release of XLA, which
is still experimental.

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:35.750
And it stands for
accelerated linear algebra,

00:26:35.750 --> 00:26:39.420
and I know everybody loves
talking about linear algebra.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:43.340
But in a nutshell, what it
does is it takes your trained

00:26:43.340 --> 00:26:44.900
graph or model--

00:26:44.900 --> 00:26:49.380
and think about
compiling that down.

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:51.140
So instead of
representing it with c++,

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:53.056
it actually represents
it with a machine code.

00:26:53.056 --> 00:26:53.737
So it makes it--

00:26:53.737 --> 00:26:55.070
MARK MANDEL: Like another layer.

00:26:55.070 --> 00:26:55.790
YUFENG GUO: Exactly.

00:26:55.790 --> 00:26:57.164
Or another layer
of optimization.

00:26:57.164 --> 00:26:59.379
So instead of running a
compiler on your code,

00:26:59.379 --> 00:27:00.920
right-- think about
compiling a code,

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:02.310
and now it's really fast, right.

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:03.851
What we're going to
do is we're going

00:27:03.851 --> 00:27:06.237
to compile the graph,
which in our analogy

00:27:06.237 --> 00:27:07.070
is kind of the code.

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:08.560
That's what the data
is flowing through.

00:27:08.560 --> 00:27:10.226
We're gonna compile
that down to machine

00:27:10.226 --> 00:27:12.110
code, or something
like machine code,

00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:13.652
so that the data
can run even faster.

00:27:13.652 --> 00:27:14.568
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:27:14.568 --> 00:27:15.440
That's very amazing.

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:17.024
MARK MANDEL: That is very cool.

00:27:17.024 --> 00:27:17.690
I'm curious now.

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:20.900
So as well, what's been
the most interesting

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:24.650
or crazy or wonderful machine
learning thing that you've seen

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:26.860
people do with either
TensorFlow or machine learning.

00:27:26.860 --> 00:27:28.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I have an
answer to that question,

00:27:28.430 --> 00:27:29.150
but you go first.

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:30.491
[LAUGHING]

00:27:30.491 --> 00:27:31.490
YUFENG GUO: Sounds good.

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:36.920
So I think one of the most
relevant in terms of real world

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:39.260
applications of machine
learning, that I've seen,

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:41.510
is the recent
research that came out

00:27:41.510 --> 00:27:48.700
around skin cancer and
diabetic retinopathy detection.

00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:52.160
And there's many groups
doing similar work

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:54.830
for other areas of medicine
as well, because these

00:27:54.830 --> 00:27:56.990
are areas where you
have a task that

00:27:56.990 --> 00:28:00.560
is highly sophisticated,
well-defined and repeatable,

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:05.150
and right now is
limited to the kind

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:10.970
of domain of trained experts
who are human and thus

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:13.820
subject to tiredness,
sleepiness,

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:15.590
grumpiness, things like that.

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:18.590
And so having a machine
that can at least do

00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:21.130
that initial first
pass for you is great.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:24.170
It's sort of like you
don't have a traffic cop

00:28:24.170 --> 00:28:26.060
standing at every single
intersection waiting

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:28.520
for someone to run
that red light.

00:28:28.520 --> 00:28:29.610
You have a camera.

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:32.030
It will flag them for
you, and then maybe you

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:34.490
have a human at the end of
the chain that says like,

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:35.540
oh no that was fine.

00:28:35.540 --> 00:28:36.560
He was turning on a red.

00:28:36.560 --> 00:28:37.820
Or something like that.

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:40.569
But you know, it helps
you get that first pass.

00:28:40.569 --> 00:28:42.610
And so in the example with
skin cancer, you know,

00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:45.230
you have very good detection
rates that are actually

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:46.852
comparable to professionals.

00:28:46.852 --> 00:28:47.810
But even if you didn't.

00:28:47.810 --> 00:28:50.460
Even for domains where
it's not perfect,

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:53.270
I think it would be
interesting to see,

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:55.880
because image recognition
and computer vision

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:59.240
are kind of more advanced than
some other areas of machine

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:00.087
learning right now.

00:29:00.087 --> 00:29:02.420
It would be really interesting
to see those applications

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:03.980
in places where it's
not as perfect, as

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:05.730
long as you can have
a human in the chain,

00:29:05.730 --> 00:29:08.420
because if it's not perfect
and you're making mistakes,

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:09.919
then it can lead
to bad experience.

00:29:09.919 --> 00:29:11.960
MARK MANDEL: I guess it
also gets interesting too

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:16.730
where personnel on ground is
probably less easy to get to

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:18.282
or able to reach to.

00:29:18.282 --> 00:29:20.240
And so if you can make
that first pass in a way

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:22.530
that can be automated,
or something like that.

00:29:22.530 --> 00:29:24.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah I can imagine,

