00:33:41.875 --> 00:33:44.250
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so I think
this is an interesting one,

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:46.290
and I think probably
right up your alley.

00:33:46.290 --> 00:33:50.430
So when talking about
like rest end points,

00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:53.400
often I hear about these
things called API keys.

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.680
So what are API
keys, and how can I

00:33:55.680 --> 00:33:57.480
use them on cloud end points?

00:33:57.480 --> 00:33:59.860
Like what would I use them
for, and why would I use them?

00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:00.693
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:34:00.693 --> 00:34:03.090
So the first part
of the question

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:04.890
is, what is an API key?

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:11.370
An API key is a key that you
use to authorize some API call.

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:15.090
And the important thing is
that this is authorization, not

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:16.810
really authentication.

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:19.770
These are not really secure.

00:34:19.770 --> 00:34:22.679
You should not use them
as a security measure

00:34:22.679 --> 00:34:26.460
really, because they're actually
kind of easy to intercept.

00:34:26.460 --> 00:34:30.150
Once they're
intercepted, they do not

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:34.560
have any expiration time.

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:37.900
So you know, it's like once you
have it, you have it forever.

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:40.409
Unless it's of
course banned, which

00:34:40.409 --> 00:34:42.389
means that for
some things they're

00:34:42.389 --> 00:34:44.370
very useful, because
they're very easy to use.

00:34:44.370 --> 00:34:46.710
Basically in general,
you just add them

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:48.960
as a parameter to a query.

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:51.780
Like very often that you
will see them on the URL.

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:54.210
They can be very
useful for service

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:56.489
to servers authentication
for instance.

00:34:56.489 --> 00:34:58.440
Specifically, when
one of the service

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:00.590
is not really behaving
in the name of a user,

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:02.340
but just as a project.

00:35:02.340 --> 00:35:05.350
You have a project acting
as a project itself.

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:06.850
That is one of
the ways to do it.

00:35:06.850 --> 00:35:08.410
Very simple to do.

00:35:08.410 --> 00:35:11.510
And especially when you
actually trust the whole chain,

00:35:11.510 --> 00:35:13.960
and is a chain like over
HTTPS or something where,

00:35:13.960 --> 00:35:18.550
basically, the content is not
shown to everyone looking.

00:35:18.550 --> 00:35:22.570
The other option is to use
authentication tokens, right,

00:35:22.570 --> 00:35:24.730
which is off to stuff like that.

00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:28.000
Which is way more advanced, but
also it's harder to maintain.

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:29.050
It's harder to use.

00:35:29.050 --> 00:35:31.546
Like, I don't know if
you've implemented off

00:35:31.546 --> 00:35:33.274
to dance on a server.

00:35:33.274 --> 00:35:33.940
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:35.231
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is not fun.

00:35:35.231 --> 00:35:36.160
It is really not fun.

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:37.576
It is the kind of
thing that makes

00:35:37.576 --> 00:35:40.900
you love Firebase authentication
stuff, like that really.

00:35:40.900 --> 00:35:43.000
So when to use API keys.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:44.480
Well, the answer
is pretty simple.

00:35:44.480 --> 00:35:48.410
The answer is whenever you
authenticating project, not

00:35:48.410 --> 00:35:50.320
a user, right,
because you don't want

00:35:50.320 --> 00:35:52.750
to have an API key per user.

00:35:52.750 --> 00:35:56.420
It's per project, per group,
of like an app for instance.

00:35:56.420 --> 00:35:58.300
If you have an
mobile app, you might

00:35:58.300 --> 00:35:59.830
want to use an API key there.

00:35:59.830 --> 00:36:01.870
And also when you're
able to make sure

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:04.780
that is actually hidden
enough, or secured enough,

00:36:04.780 --> 00:36:07.240
that people will be
able to find it easy.

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:11.050
And no matter when
you're using an API key,

00:36:11.050 --> 00:36:15.670
those APIs keys should never
be the only security measure

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:16.360
that you have.

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:16.990
MARK MANDEL: All right, cool.

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:18.880
So it sounds like
API keys are just

00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:21.100
a nice, light way to give
people access to your APIs,

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:23.870
but also be able to determine
who is accessing your APIs,

00:36:23.870 --> 00:36:26.956
unlike on one of behalf they
are accessing your APIs.

00:36:26.956 --> 00:36:28.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, basically.

00:36:28.330 --> 00:36:31.120
So if you want to control
like security, like hardcore

00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:33.140
security, it's not
the way to do it.

00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:36.850
But if you want to control
things like quotas or basically

00:36:36.850 --> 00:36:41.434
having a better way of tracking
who is using what, something

00:36:41.434 --> 00:36:43.600
that you can grab from the
logs and stuff like that,

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:44.642
API key's great for that.

00:36:44.642 --> 00:36:45.600
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:47.830
And so cloud end points,
they give you an easy way

00:36:47.830 --> 00:36:49.300
to set up API keys,
is that right?

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:51.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh
yes, it's super simple.

00:36:51.800 --> 00:36:53.860
So the cool thing is
that basically you can--

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:57.690
once you deploy cloud end
points API on your project,

00:36:57.690 --> 00:37:01.240
then only the API keys
created from a project

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:04.450
that have access to
that API are valid.

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:07.420
Which means that then what you
can do is you create your API,

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:10.840
you deploy it, and then
you create as many API keys

00:37:10.840 --> 00:37:12.910
as you want from your
project directly,

00:37:12.910 --> 00:37:16.270
and then you can share
those, which is very simple.

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:18.430
Or even better,
you share your API

00:37:18.430 --> 00:37:22.360
with whoever you want to
give access to your API

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:25.450
then they activate their
API on their project

00:37:25.450 --> 00:37:27.430
like you would
activate any other API,

00:37:27.430 --> 00:37:32.830
like let's say Compute Engine
or Kubernets, Container Engine.

00:37:32.830 --> 00:37:34.780
All of those are different APIs.

00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:36.460
Just go to the API manager.

00:37:36.460 --> 00:37:39.460
You activate it, and then
you can create a new API

00:37:39.460 --> 00:37:41.360
from that project directly.

00:37:41.360 --> 00:37:45.730
So basically you're
able to expose an API as

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:47.820
if you were Google, basically.

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:51.760
And anyone that has access to
that API can create an API key,

00:37:51.760 --> 00:37:53.860
and get direct
access to your API.

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:54.610
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:55.510
Sounds great.

00:37:55.510 --> 00:37:57.570
I can't wait to get
started with API keys.

